      "id": 1,
      "text": "Farrell sprang up overnight in 1901 when a steel mill was built on a plain by the Shenango River, earning the nickname \"The Magic City\".",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        19
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Farrell%2C_Pennsylvania",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The Roemer Works, later known as Farrell Works, was the town’s lifeblood until its 1992 liquidation, after which Caparo and Duferco took over.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        24,
        25
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Farrell%2C_Pennsylvania",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In 1939 artist Virginia Wood Riggs painted the mural \"Myths of Vulcan and Juno\" in Farrell’s post office, only to have it covered in 1966.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        22,
        23
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Farrell%2C_Pennsylvania",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Farrell sits along the Shenango River in western Mercer County, part of the Hermitage micropolitan area.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        17,
        18
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
